-- allium: 3
-- launch-objective.allium
--
-- Scope: The economic decision contract for cloud launch planning — the
-- objective every launch candidate is scored by, the models that feed it,
-- the grouping / reuse / count decisions it drives, its fallbacks when models
-- are absent, and the deliberate non-goals of the current planner.
--
-- This spec describes the DECISION CONTRACT, not the estimator internals.
-- Formula constants, priors, decay half-lives, and profile weight values live
-- in code behind @guidance pointers.
--
-- Includes:
--   - The scalarized cost+time launch objective (LaunchObjective)
--   - Grouping / reuse / instance-count decisions scored under that objective
--   - The survival and setup-overhead models that feed the objective, and
--     when they are (re)built
--   - Deterministic fallbacks when no model can be built
--   - The precise distinction between launch scoring and queue rebalance
--   - Deliberate current limitations of the planner
-- Excludes:
--   - Statistical estimator internals: distributions, priors, decay
--     constants, profile weight VALUES (implementation detail in
--     internal/bidding and internal/estimate)
--   - Instance lifecycle from launch through termination
--     (campaign-lifecycle.allium)
--   - On-prem host scoring and eligibility (inventory-placement.allium)
--   - Offer search UI and TUI rendering

-- ==========================================================================
-- External entities
-- ==========================================================================
--
-- These are governed elsewhere; this spec references them to describe the
-- decision, not to define them.

-- A queued unit of work the planner is trying to place. Governed by
-- job-lifecycle.allium.
external entity Job {
    gpu_mem_gb: Decimal?
}

-- A running or grace-period cloud rental that may be reused. Governed by
-- campaign-lifecycle.allium (entity Instance).
external entity Instance {
    running_job_count: Integer
}

-- A live provider offer for a fresh rental. Maps to campaign-lifecycle.allium
-- value OfferSnapshot.
external entity Offer {
    cost_per_hour_cents: Integer
    reliability: Decimal
}

-- ==========================================================================
-- Value types
-- ==========================================================================

-- A named cost/time weighting. Its weight VALUES live in code
-- (internal/bidding/model.go strategyWeights); only the shape is specified
-- here. Profile ids: cheap, balanced, fast, fastest, plus the Pareto sweep.
value ScoreProfile {
    id: String                  -- cheap | balanced | fast | fastest | pareto-*
    cost_weight: Decimal        -- weight on expected dollar cost
    time_weight: Decimal        -- weight on expected aggregate completion time
    happy_path: Bool            -- true for profiles that do NOT survival-divide
                                -- the time term (e.g. fastest)
}

-- One thing the planner can rank: a fresh-launch grouping placed on an offer,
-- or the assignment of a group to an already-running reusable instance.
value LaunchCandidate {
    kind: new_launch | reuse
    jobs: List<Job>             -- the group scored as a unit
    offer: Offer?               -- present for kind = new_launch
    instance: Instance?         -- present for kind = reuse
}

-- ==========================================================================
-- The launch objective
-- ==========================================================================

contract LaunchObjective {
    score: (candidate: LaunchCandidate, profile: ScoreProfile) -> Decimal
        -- The single scalarized objective every launch candidate — new-instance
        -- grouping, reuse assignment, and offer — is ranked by, under the
        -- active profile:
        --   profile.cost_weight * expected_cost(candidate, profile)
        -- + profile.time_weight * expected_completion(candidate, profile)
        -- Lower is better.
    expected_cost: (candidate: LaunchCandidate, profile: ScoreProfile) -> Decimal
        -- Retry- and survival-adjusted expected dollar spend for the candidate.
    expected_completion: (candidate: LaunchCandidate, profile: ScoreProfile) -> Decimal
        -- Survival-adjusted aggregate job-completion time, in hours, over the
        -- candidate's whole job set.

    @invariant BothTermsSurvivalAdjusted
        -- Neither term is a happy-path point estimate. Expected cost is
        -- retry-adjusted: a failed attempt's setup spend recurs with the
        -- attempt's failure probability, so unreliable offers cost more than
        -- their nominal $/hr. Expected completion divides by survival
        -- probability, EXCEPT for profiles whose profile.happy_path is true
        -- (e.g. `fastest`), which score the happy-path wall-clock time by
        -- design.

    @invariant TimeTermIsAggregate
        -- The time term is aggregate over the group's jobs, not per-job.
        -- Setup overhead is counted once per instance, and each job on a
        -- shared instance waits sequentially behind the jobs ahead of it in
        -- the group's queue. A grouping that packs more jobs onto one instance
        -- amortizes setup but lengthens later jobs' completion; the objective
        -- prices both effects.

    @invariant RequiredFactorsParticipate
        -- Every score must incorporate, at minimum: the overhead-model setup
        -- estimate, the survival probability for the candidate's bucket,
        -- per-group amortization of setup, and predicted per-job runtime (from
        -- the estimator when a prediction is available, from a duration
        -- fallback otherwise). Dropping any of these silently biases the
        -- objective and is a defect, not a simplification.

    @invariant ActiveProfileRecorded
        -- The active profile's id is recorded on placement telemetry for every
        -- scored candidate, so a decision can be reconstructed against the
        -- exchange rate that produced it.

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: internal/bidding/model.go — ScoreProfile (weights),
        -- ExpectedCost (retry-adjusted spend), ExpectedWallclockTime, and
        -- aggregateJobCompletionTimeHrs (setup-once, sequential per-job wait).
        -- internal/campaign/strategy_plan.go ScoreEstimatesWithProfile and
        -- BestCandidateForProfile rank candidates; internal/campaign/offers.go
        -- bestOfferWithCompatibilityProfile picks the offer for a group under
        -- the profile. Profile ids and the Pareto sweep:
        -- StrategyCheap/StrategyFast/StrategyFastest and ParetoSamplingProfiles
        -- in internal/bidding/model.go. Active-profile telemetry:
        -- internal/campaign/telemetry.go and
        -- internal/db/scheduling_telemetry.go.
}

-- ==========================================================================
-- Decisions scored under the objective
-- ==========================================================================

rule GroupingCandidatesScoredUnderObjective {
    when: RentalLaunchSelection(unplaced_jobs)
          or AutoPilotTick(unplaced_jobs)
          or MoveToNewLaunchSelection(jobs)

    -- The planner enumerates alternative groupings of the compatible jobs —
    -- split into parallel single-job instances, merged onto shared instances,
    -- and intermediate affinity groupings — and scores each grouping's
    -- candidates under the same LaunchObjective. The lowest-scoring candidate
    -- wins.
    let candidates = CandidateGroupings(unplaced_jobs)

    ensures:
        SelectedGrouping = argmin LaunchObjective.score(candidate, active_profile)
                                  over candidates
        -- jobs-per-instance is an OUTPUT of this argmin, never a fixed cap:
        -- the objective's setup amortization vs. sequential-wait tradeoff
        -- decides how many jobs share an instance.
        JobsPerInstance(SelectedGrouping) is not bounded by a fixed constant

    @guidance
        -- The shared-input setup benefit that biases grouping toward
        -- co-locating jobs with overlapping HF model/dataset inputs is
        -- specified in campaign-lifecycle.allium AssetOverlapLaunchGrouping;
        -- it is a scoring term of this same objective, not a separate gate.
        -- Implementation: internal/campaign/offers.go FetchCandidateGroupings
        -- enumerates candidates; internal/campaign/campaign.go
        -- MergeCompatibleGroups / SplitToParallel and GroupByAffinity produce
        -- the grouping alternatives that are then scored.
}

rule ReuseOnlyWhenItBeatsNewLaunch {
    when: BuildAutoPlacementPlan(unplaced_jobs)

    -- A reusable instance is assigned to a group iff its objective score beats
    -- the best fresh-launch alternative for that group under the active
    -- profile. Reuse is not preferred merely because it is zero-launch-cost;
    -- it must win on the objective.
    ensures:
        for group in groups_to_place:
            let best_reuse = min LaunchObjective.score(reuse_candidate, active_profile)
                                 over ReuseCandidates(group)
            let best_new = min LaunchObjective.score(new_candidate, active_profile)
                               over NewLaunchCandidates(group)
            AssignReuse(group) only if best_reuse < best_new

        -- Queue depth on a reusable instance enters the objective as a longer
        -- expected completion (sequential wait), NEVER as an eligibility cap.
        -- See campaign-lifecycle.allium InstanceReuseQueueDepthIsObjectiveSignal.
        ReuseEligibility(group, instance) does_not_reject because queue_depth alone

        -- Scarcity / opportunity cost: when other queued compatible groups
        -- also demand the reusable supply, assigning this scarce instance
        -- carries an opportunity-cost penalty in the objective, so a group
        -- that could launch fresh does not monopolize reuse another group
        -- needs more.
        CompatibleReuseDemand(group) > CompatibleReuseSupply(group) implies
            LaunchObjective.score(reuse_candidate, active_profile)
                includes a scarcity opportunity-cost penalty

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: internal/campaign/strategy_plan.go chooseReuseGroups
        -- compares reuse against fresh-launch candidates and applies
        -- reuseOpportunityPenaltyUSD when demand for compatible reusable
        -- capacity exceeds supply. Reuse candidate discovery and matching:
        -- internal/campaign/reuse.go FindReusableInstances, MatchJobToInstance
        -- (queue depth is a completion-time signal, not a reject), and
        -- rankBetter.
}

-- ==========================================================================
-- The models that feed the objective
-- ==========================================================================

rule ModelsRebuiltFromHistory {
    when: AutoPilotTick(_)
          or RentalLaunchSelection(_)
          or RelaunchOrphanedJob(_)
          or UserRequestsLaunch(_)

    -- The survival model and the setup-overhead model are rebuilt from
    -- persisted history at each planning invocation. Planning never consumes a
    -- stale cached model artifact carried across passes; a pass sees the
    -- outcomes recorded since the previous one.
    ensures:
        survival_model = BuildSurvivalModel(LoadInstanceOutcomes())
        overhead_model = BuildOverheadModel(QueryOverheadObservations())

        -- Survival training excludes non-signal outcomes. An
        -- account_credit_exhausted termination says nothing about the machine,
        -- SKU, or region and must not depress a bucket's survival estimate.
        SurvivalTrainingSet excludes outcomes classified account_credit_exhausted

    @guidance
        -- Survival training and its outcome classification:
        -- internal/bidding/build.go LoadInstanceOutcomes and
        -- ClassifySurvivalTrainingOutcome (which drops the credit-exhaustion
        -- class — see campaign-lifecycle.allium ProviderCreditGate and the
        -- account_credit_exhausted TerminationReason prose). Overhead model:
        -- internal/estimate/overhead_build.go BuildOverheadModel over
        -- observations from launches timestamps and job_phase_timings via
        -- internal/db/overhead_stats.go QueryOverheadObservations. Per-pass
        -- build call sites: internal/orchestration/relaunch.go
        -- buildOverheadModel and cmd/instance_launch.go buildOverheadModel /
        -- LoadInstanceOutcomes.
}

rule FallbacksWhenModelsAbsent {
    when: ScoreCandidate(candidate, active_profile)

    -- Fallbacks are deterministic and must never block planning: an absent
    -- model degrades the estimate, it does not veto a launch. This mirrors
    -- inventory-placement.allium PredictorNeverBlocksPlacement.
    ensures:
        not exists overhead_model implies
            SetupEstimate(candidate) = ConstantSetupFallback

        not exists SurvivalHistory(candidate.bucket) implies
            SurvivalPrior(candidate.bucket) = seeded from candidate.offer.reliability

        Planning does_not_block because a model was absent

    @guidance
        -- Constant setup fallback: bidding.ConstantSetup(0.5) applied in
        -- internal/campaign/offers.go and internal/campaign/strategy_plan.go
        -- when no OverheadModel can be built. Survival prior seeding: the
        -- Beta-Binomial prior in internal/bidding/model.go seeds an offer
        -- bucket with no history from the provider-advertised reliability
        -- (PriorStrength pseudo-observations). The exact constant, prior
        -- strength, and shrinkage are code-level; only the "deterministic,
        -- never blocks" contract is specified here.
}

-- ==========================================================================
-- Invariants
-- ==========================================================================

invariant LaunchAndRebalanceObjectivesAreDistinct {
    -- Launch planning and queue rebalance use DIFFERENT objectives today, and
    -- any claim that they are the same is wrong.
    --
    -- Launch planning ranks candidates by the weighted-sum LaunchObjective
    -- above: cost_weight * expected_cost + time_weight * expected_completion.
    --
    -- Queue rebalance (campaign-lifecycle.allium § Queue re-balance) ranks
    -- moves by a LEXICOGRAPHIC objective:
    --   1. priority completion sum, 2. total completion sum,
    --   3. total rental cost, 4. scalar score, 5. global makespan.
    -- The two objectives share only the ScoreProfile dollar/hour weights, and
    -- only inside the lexicographic objective's scalar-score component (4).
    invariant_holds:
        LaunchPlanningObjective = weighted_sum(cost_weight, time_weight)
        and RebalanceObjective = lexicographic(priority_completion,
                                               total_completion,
                                               rental_cost,
                                               scalar_score,
                                               global_makespan)
        and Shared(LaunchPlanningObjective, RebalanceObjective)
                = { ScoreProfile.cost_weight, ScoreProfile.time_weight }
                  used only in RebalanceObjective.scalar_score

    @guidance
        -- Launch scoring: internal/bidding/model.go +
        -- internal/campaign/strategy_plan.go (see LaunchObjective above).
        -- Rebalance scoring: internal/orchestration/rebalance.go
        -- (LexicographicObjective / ScoreUnder). If these two are ever
        -- unified, this invariant and campaign-lifecycle.allium § Queue
        -- re-balance must be updated together.
}

-- ==========================================================================
-- Deliberate non-goals and known limitations
-- ==========================================================================
--
-- Each item below is a CURRENT limitation recorded on purpose, so a gap
-- analysis reads intent rather than inferring an oversight.
--
-- 1. No historical stock-availability model. Offers are fetched live each
--    pass and cached only within that pass; there is no persisted offer /
--    market-price history and no model of how likely a SKU is to be
--    available. Unavailability is handled reactively: a failed fetch is
--    "unknown", not "empty" (see campaign-lifecycle.allium
--    OfferFetchFailureIsUnknownNotEmpty). Planned work: telemetry collection
--    in lab-notebook/plans/2026-07-12-launch-objective-now.md; the model in
--    lab-notebook/plans/backlog/2026-07-12-scheduler-economics-later.md
--    Part 1.
--
-- 2. No wait / defer action. Every pass either launches now or blocks with a
--    reason; there is no option-value-of-waiting model that would hold a job
--    back expecting cheaper or more-available capacity soon. Planned work:
--    lab-notebook/plans/backlog/2026-07-12-scheduler-economics-later.md
--    Part 2.
--
-- 3. The newsvendor instance-count recommendation is advisory display only,
--    not a planner input. It surfaces the count implied by uncertain demand
--    and the active profile to the user; the autopilot planner does not
--    consume it. See campaign-lifecycle.allium NewsvendorLaunchRecommendation.
--
-- 4. Market prices are deliberately excluded from spend-authorization
--    ceilings. The authorization gate uses per-job / per-class overrides and a
--    price anchor, not the live market median (which is informational only).
--    See campaign-lifecycle.allium PriceAuthorizationGate.
--
-- 5. Group disk is sized for the whole group's inputs for the instance's whole
--    life; there is no per-phase disk / eviction model that would let
--    sequential jobs on one instance reclaim space between phases. See
--    campaign-lifecycle.allium FreshRentalDiskEstimate.
--
-- 6. The amortization deferrals listed in campaign-lifecycle.allium § Queue
--    re-balance under "Logistics intentionally deferred" (same-disk affinity
--    for --produces / --needs pairs, HF model/dataset runtime-cost
--    amortization beyond the provisioned-input heuristic, artifact-staging
--    amortization, and uv package-cache setup-time reuse) apply to launch
--    scoring as well.

-- ==========================================================================
-- Cross-references
-- ==========================================================================
--
-- campaign-lifecycle.allium owns cloud instance lifecycle (launch through
-- termination), the AssetOverlapLaunchGrouping setup-benefit term, the
-- InstanceReuseQueueDepthIsObjectiveSignal reuse-penalty rule, the
-- lexicographic queue-rebalance objective (§ Queue re-balance), the
-- NewsvendorLaunchRecommendation advisory, PriceAuthorizationGate,
-- FreshRentalDiskEstimate, and OfferFetchFailureIsUnknownNotEmpty. This spec
-- references those anchors but does not redefine them.
--
-- inventory-placement.allium owns on-prem host scoring and eligibility, and
-- the PredictorNeverBlocksPlacement invariant this spec's fallback rule
-- mirrors for the cloud side.
--
-- job-lifecycle.allium governs Job status and the queue this planner draws
-- unplaced work from.
